<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">River Tweed</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The River Tweed flows about 156 km from the Southern Uplands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berwick-upon-tweed">Berwick-upon-Tweed</a>. For part of its lower course it marks the border between Scotland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. The lands between the Tweed and the Forth, once part of Northumbria, passed to the Scottish kings in the 10th and 11th centuries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/river-tweed.jpg" alt="River Tweed" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Iain Lees · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_River_Tweed_at_Abbotsford_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1545874.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
